<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="5">

  <Content>
    <Paragraph Number="1" Type="Scene Heading">
      <SceneProperties Length="1 2/8" Page="1" Title="" Summary="Wren cuts a key for a stranger who won't say whose door it opens.">
        <SceneArcBeats/>
      </SceneProperties>
      <Text AdornmentStyle="0" Background="#FFFFFFFFFFFF" Color="#000000000000" Font="Courier Final Draft" RevisionID="0" Size="12" Style="">EXT. KWIK KEYS KIOSK - SHOPPING ARCADE - DAY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Left" FirstIndent="0.00" Leading="Regular" LeftIndent="1.50" RightIndent="7.50" SpaceBefore="12" Spacing="1" StartsNewPage="No" Type="Action">
      <Text AdornmentStyle="0" Background="#FFFFFFFFFFFF" Color="#000000000000" Font="Courier Final Draft" RevisionID="0" Size="12" Style="">A booth the size of a phone box, wedged between a nail bar and a shuttered bakery. Inside, </Text>
      <Text AdornmentStyle="0" Background="#FFFFFFFFFFFF" Color="#000000000000" Font="Courier Final Draft" RevisionID="0" Size="12" Style="Bold">WREN MADDOX</Text>
      <Text AdornmentStyle="0" Background="#FFFFFFFFFFFF" Color="#000000000000" Font="Courier Final Draft" RevisionID="0" Size="12" Style=""> (40s, reading glasses on a chain, a burn scar on one thumb) files a blank against the grinder.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A MAN IN A WET RAINCOAT (60s) sets a single brass key on the counter. Old. </Text>
      <Text Style="Italic">Hand-cut.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>WREN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Two copies or one?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAINCOAT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(not looking at her)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>One. And I'll wait.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Shot">
      <Text>CLOSE ON THE KEY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A tiny anchor is stamped where the maker's mark should be. Wren turns it over. Her face gives nothing away. Her thumb does: it </Text>
      <Text Style="Underline">stops</Text>
      <Text> on the anchor.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>WREN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Where did you get this?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAINCOAT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Does it matter?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>WREN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>My dad made this. He's been </Text>
      <Text Style="Bold+Italic">dead</Text>
      <Text> nine years.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph Number="2" Type="Scene Heading">
      <SceneProperties Length="1 3/8" Page="2" Title="" Summary="Wren and her nephew Dov argue over the key; both of them want to try it.">
        <SceneArcBeats/>
      </SceneProperties>
      <Text>INT. WREN'S FLAT - KITCHEN - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Cramped. A radiator ticks. </Text>
      <Text Style="Bold">DOV</Text>
      <Text> (17), all elbows, eats cereal out of a mixing bowl. The brass key sits between them on a saucer, like evidence.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>DOV</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>So you just kept his key.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>WREN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I kept the </Text>
      <Text Style="Italic">copy</Text>
      <Text>. He's got the original.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>DOV</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(mouth full)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And you didn't follow him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She didn't. She hates that she didn't.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>WREN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Granddad stamped that anchor on one lock in his whole life. The boathouse at Fenwick Reach.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
      <DualDialogue>
        <Paragraph Type="Character">
          <Text>DOV</Text>
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
          <Text>We're going tonight.</Text>
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph Type="Character">
          <Text>WREN</Text>
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
          <Text>We are </Text>
          <Text Style="Bold+Underline">not</Text>
          <Text> going tonight.</Text>
        </Paragraph>
      </DualDialogue>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A beat. Wren reaches for her coat anyway.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>SMASH CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph Number="3" StartsNewPage="Yes" Type="Scene Heading">
      <SceneProperties Length="1 6/8" Page="3" Title="The boathouse" Summary="The key fits. Someone has been living inside.">
        <SceneArcBeats/>
      </SceneProperties>
      <Text>EXT. FENWICK REACH - BOATHOUSE - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Wind off the estuary. A long timber shed on stilts, half its windows boarded. Wren's torch finds the padlock: brass, green with age, the same anchor.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Dov holds the torch. Wren slides the key home. It turns like silk.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>DOV</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(whispering)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Somebody oiled that. Recently.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="General">
      <Text>The SOUND of water slapping the pilings, close.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Inside: a camp bed, made with hospital corners. A kettle on a gas ring. On the far wall, dozens of keys hang on nails, each with a paper tag.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Shot">
      <Text>ANGLE ON THE TAGS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Every tag carries a date in the same careful hand. The last one is </Text>
      <Text Style="Bold+Italic+Underline">today's</Text>
      <Text>.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>WREN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(barely)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That's his writing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph Number="4" Type="Scene Heading">
      <SceneProperties Length="1 4/8" Page="4" Title=""/>
      <Text>INT. BOATHOUSE - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A floorboard creaks behind them. The man in the raincoat stands in the doorway, a carrier bag of groceries in one hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAINCOAT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You've got his hands. I watched you file that blank.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>WREN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Who are you?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAINCOAT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I kept his locks working. He asked me to, at the end. Somebody had to know where all the doors were.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He holds out his palm. Wren looks at the key, at Dov, at the wall of tags.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She puts the key in his hand. Then she takes a blank from her coat pocket and sets it beside it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>WREN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Teach me the rest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>FADE OUT.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
  </Content>

